hi everyone and welcome to biology  professor  today we're talking about photosynthesis  and cell respiration  basically we're going to look at how  those two are connected  and what they have in common now they're  actually a lot more connected  than most people think they're part of a  larger  cycle that starts with energy that comes  from the sun  that sun provides light energy that is  absorbed by  chloroplasts  this is where photosynthesis happens  photosynthesis  yields two types of products first  organic molecules  the most common you'll hear about is  glucose so a type of sugar  the other major product yielded by  photosynthesis  is oxygen or o2  organic molecules like glucose and  oxygen  are then used by mitochondria and  cellular respiration  this is where the mitochondria has  enzymes present  that through several complicated steps  break down organic molecules use oxygen  as a final electron acceptor to generate  something very very important atp  atp is basically the energy  currency of the cell it's like a dollar  bill  everywhere in the cell takes it and this  is where energy is stored  that the cell can later use for a  variety of  cellular processes now where does the  cycle  part come in in mitochondria with cell  respiration  once they have used the organic  molecules and the oxygen  there are two different products that  are formed these are  carbon dioxide and water  carbon dioxide and water are the two  reactants that chloroplasts need to  carry out photosynthesis  so that is how this is one big cycle  now let's look at what these processes  photosynthesis and cell respiration what  do they have in common  well they both use electron transport  chains  electron transport chains are these  series of enzymes that are part of  biological  membranes that pass electrons  from place to place within that chain  and  in so doing they harvest energy from  those electrons  and create something called a proton  gradient the proton gradient is then  used  in both photosynthesis and cell  respiration  by atp synthase  and this is another common misconception  people think that atp  is only involved in cell respiration and  it's true that cell respiration is where  the cell  gets the atp that it needs for all of  its different  functions but photosynthesis has atp  synthase as well  in photosynthesis atp synthase is used  to generate atp in an early step of  photosynthesis  that is then used up in a later step of  photosynthesis  where the organic molecules are being  made so  atp is a part of both of these things  also both both photosynthesis  and cell respiration occur in plants  algae and photosynthetic bacteria  people are tempted to say that  autotrophs like plants use  photosynthesis  and heterotrophs like animals use cell  respiration  that's true but actually plants use  both and algae and photosynthetic  bacteria  they use both they use the  photosynthesis to  generate the organic molecules and the  oxygen and they use the mitochondria  that they have  for cell respiration to get the atp that  they need  for all of their various functions  now i also want to mention as a side  note that photosynthetic bacteria  you might be saying well how can they  have photosynthesis  they don't have chloroplasts that's true  bacteria are prokaryotic cells and they  lack organelles  so they don't have chloroplasts they  don't have mitochondria  instead the enzymes that are used in  photosynthesis  and cell respiration they have as a part  of their plasma  membrane instead of being specifically  in the cell in these compartmentalized  places  now another thing that the two processes  have in common is that they use  biological membranes for increased  surface area  what does this mean well cells  are very small places and they have a  lot going on  inside of them you see that chloroplasts  have this double membrane system  and then on the inside they've got more  membranes the membranes here are in  black and the mitochondria  also is a double membrane you've got the  outer membrane and then you've got this  inner membrane that has  lots and lots of folds these folds are  called  cristae  in chloroplasts all of these individual  little ovals  are called thylakoids  and the thylakoids when stacked together  in a column like this  form what's called a granum  but regardless of the terminology in  both cases you have a lot  of biological membrane that is folded up  into a very little space and it's these  biological membranes  the thylakoid membranes and the cresting  membranes that hold those electron  transport chains  where a big portion of photosynthesis  and cell respiration  is happening so by having all of these  folds or all of these stacks of  thylakoids  this provides a lot of increased surface  area  so that photosynthesis and cell  respiration can happen  at a very high rate there's a lot of it  going on at once  because it has all of this surface area  to work with  even though it's in these small  compartments and finally another place  that photosynthesis and cell respiration  are  linked is in the endosymbiotic theory  this theory deals with the evolution of  eukaryotes  so eukaryotes as different from  prokaryotes  eukaryotes are these cells that have a  lot of  membrane bound organelles so organelles  like chloroplasts like mitochondria  but also other organelles as well  lysosomes  endoplasmic reticulum the golgi  apparatus  the nucleus all of these are specific  to eukaryotes the endosymbiotic theory  says that mitochondria  and chloroplasts are special though that  they were formed  when an early ancestor of eukaryotic  cells  engulfed a photosynthetic prokaryote  that then became a chloroplast and a  non-photosynthetic prokaryote that then  became a mitochondrion  and that these two organelles come  from this ancient  symbiosis that was formed when  prokaryotes were  engulfed by an early eukaryote so that  is  one other thing that photosynthesis and  cell respiration have  in common that is it for today i hope  you enjoyed and thanks for watching
 

